"YouTube Copyright Transparency Report"],["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.MastheadTitleDescription",'YouTube offers a suite of copyright management tools that gives rightsholders control of their copyrighted material on YouTube. These tools have allowed creativity to thrive, while giving creators and artists the tools and resources needed to manage their content online. The Copyright Transparency Report shares information about the copyright enforcement efforts on YouTube. The report covers <a href="/youtube-copyright/everyone-has-access?{localeParam}">Everyone Has Access</a>, <a href="/youtube-copyright/balanced-ecosystem?{localeParam}">Creating a Balanced Ecosystem</a>, and <a href="/youtube-copyright/cutting-edge?{localeParam}">Our Tools Are Cutting Edge</a>.'],
["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.MastheadTitle","Highlights from the YouTube Copyright Transparency Report"],["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.MastheadDescription","We build tools that empower users to access, create, and share information like never before \u2014 giving them more choice, opportunity, and exposure to a diversity of viewpoints. <b>Today, billions of logged-in users come to YouTube every month and in 2023, more people created content on YouTube than ever before.</b> YouTube has also given rightsholders opportunities to earn money from both their own and user-generated content through our subscription-based and advertising-supported models."],
["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.MastheadDescription2","YouTube has built a suite of Copyright Management tools for all rightsholders and matches them with the appropriate tool based on the scale of their copyrighted work on YouTube and the resources they have dedicated to operate the tools."],["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.MastheadDescription3","As we work to expand access to more powerful tools in the YouTube Copyright Management Suite, we must balance the need to protect creators, viewers, and rightsholders from the significant disruption that can result from the abuse or otherwise invalid use of our tools."],
["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.MastheadDescription4","We have invested hundreds of millions of dollars to develop powerful tools to help rightsholders manage their content on YouTube. We have also accumulated over a decade of learnings on how to best balance the needs of rightsholders and creators, and continue to iterate on our tools accordingly."],["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.Tab1","Introduction"],["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.Tab2","Everyone Has Access"],["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.Tab3",
"Creating a Balanced Ecosystem"],["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.Tab4","Our Tools Are Cutting Edge"],["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.Tab5","Summary"],["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.intro.SectionTitle0Component0ParagraphText","At YouTube, supporting the free flow of ideas and creativity is core to our mission to give everyone a voice and show them the world. With this in mind, we build tools that empower users to access, create, and share information like never before \u2014 giving them more choice, opportunity, and exposure to a diversity of viewpoints. Today, billions of logged-in users come to YouTube every month and in 2023, more people created content on YouTube than ever before."],
["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.intro.SectionTitle0Component1ParagraphText","YouTube has also given rightsholders opportunities to earn money from both their own and user-uploaded content through our subscription-based and advertising-supported models. Our partnerships with record labels, performing rights organizations, music publishers, television networks, and movie studios generate substantial revenues for the creative industries. YouTube paid more than $70 billion to creators, artists, and media companies in the 3 years prior to January 2024."],
["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.intro.SectionTitle0Component1ParagraphTextV8","YouTube has also given rightsholders opportunities to earn money from both their own and user-uploaded content through our subscription-based and advertising-supported models. Our partnerships with record labels, performing rights organizations, music publishers, television networks, and movie studios generate substantial revenues for the creative industries. YouTube paid more than $100 billion to creators, artists, and media companies in the 4 years prior to January 2025."],
["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.intro.SectionTitle0Component2ParagraphText","YouTube has over 100 million Premium and Music subscribers, including trialers, across 100 countries as of January 2024. Through Content ID, YouTube has created an entirely new revenue stream from ad-supported, user-generated content, paying more than $9 billion to rightsholders from ads alone as of December 2022, from content claimed and monetized through the tool."],["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.intro.SectionTitle0Group3Component0ChartTitle",
"Paid to creators, artists, and media companies over the 3 years prior to January 2024"],["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.intro.SectionTitle0Group3Component0ChartTitleV8","Paid to creators, artists, and media companies over the 4 years prior to January 2025"],["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.intro.SectionTitle0Group3Component1ChartTitle","Ad revenue paid to rightsholders as of December 2022 from content claimed and monetized through Content ID"],["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.intro.SectionTitle1",
"YouTube\u2019s Copyright Management Suite"],["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.intro.SectionTitle1Component0ParagraphText","Three main tools make up our Copyright Management Suite: the webform, the Copyright Match Tool, and Content ID. All of these tools use technology to prevent the reupload of matching content. We have invested hundreds of millions of dollars to develop and operate these tools."],["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.intro.SectionTitle2Component0ParagraphText",'All rightsholders have access to the <a href="https://www.youtube.com/copyright_complaint_form?contact_type=hc">webform</a>, which is a streamlined and efficient way to submit copyright removal requests, and is available in 80 languages. It is designed for infrequent use by creators who hold few copyrights and rarely find their content on YouTube. For the vast majority of rightsholders, the webform is the only tool they need. Nevertheless, creators who have used the webform to remove videos from YouTube have access to powerful features, including the ability to ask YouTube to automatically prevent copies of the removed videos from being reuploaded. Copyright Match Tool will also find potential copies of videos removed through the webform and provide those matches to the claimant.'],
["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.intro.SectionTitle2Component1ImageFootnote","A portion of the interface for the webform."],["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.intro.SectionTitle2Component2ParagraphTextV7",'Partners with access to Content ID can convert any match (see Content ID below) into a copyright removal request through an "Enterprise Webform" within their Studio Content Manager interface. The Enterprise Webform is a streamlined version of the public webform for experienced claimants who submit large volumes of removal requests for videos that they\u2019ve found to be potentially infringing their copyright. Separately, a small number of rightsholders and anti-piracy vendors have access to this Enterprise Webform through the Content Verification Program (CVP).'],
["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.intro.SectionTitle2Component2ParagraphTextV8",'Partners with access to Content ID can convert any match (see Content ID below) into a copyright removal request through an "Enterprise Webform" within their Studio Content Manager interface. The Enterprise Webform is a streamlined version of the public webform for experienced claimants who submit large volumes of removal requests for videos that they\u2019ve found to be potentially infringing their copyright. Separately, a small number of rightsholders and anti-piracy vendors have access to this Enterprise Webform through the Enterprise Copyright Match Tool, which replaced the Content Verification Program (CVP) in early 2025. In addition to facilitating high volume removal request submission, the Enterprise Copyright Match Tool also provides automated detection of matches to videos these users have previously removed.'],
["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.intro.SectionTitle3Group0Component0ParagraphText","After extensive collaboration with creators, we found there was a segment of rightsholders who experienced a higher amount of reposting of their copyrighted content and needed to submit more frequent copyright removal requests. With this in mind, we built the Copyright Match Tool to safely bring the power of Content ID matching technology (described in detail below) to more creators and rightsholders."],["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.intro.SectionTitle3Group0Component1ChartTitle",
"Channels on YouTube have access to the Copyright Match Tool"],["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.intro.SectionTitle3Component1ParagraphText","The Copyright Match Tool is available to any YouTube user who has submitted a valid copyright removal request through the webform. Once a takedown request is approved, the Copyright Match Tool starts scanning YouTube uploads for potential matches to the videos reported in the removal request. The tool surfaces these potential matches to the claimant so they can decide what action to take next."],
["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.intro.SectionTitle3Component2ParagraphText",'For creators in the YouTube Partner Program (YPP), or any channel that has filled out the <a target="_blank" href="https://support.google.com/youtube/contact/copyright_management_tools_form">copyright management tools application</a> and shown a need for an advanced rights management tool, the Copyright Match Tool will also scan for potential matches of their videos on other YouTube channels. Creators who have access to this feature of the Copyright Match Tool simply need to be the first to upload a video to YouTube (they can upload as public, private, or unlisted).'],
["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.intro.SectionTitle3Component3ParagraphText","For each video match, the Copyright Match Tool shows the user information on total views, the channel that uploaded it, the percentage of the video that is made of their content, and a few frames of the video. The tool also labels whether only the audio portion of the video generated a match, or if it's only a partial match, meaning content may have been sampled but the video is not a simple re-upload. From this interface, users can choose to archive the match and leave the video up, file a takedown request (with the option to ask YouTube to automatically prevent copies), or contact the uploader."],
["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.intro.SectionTitle4Component0ParagraphText",'This is our solution for those with the most complex rights management needs, such as movie studios, record labels, and collecting societies. Smaller, independent creators who do not meet the <a target="_balnk" href="https://support.google.com/youtube/answer/1311402">eligibility criteria</a> for Content ID can still access these features through a number of <a target="_blank" href="https://servicesdirectory.withyoutube.com/">service providers</a>. These service providers manage rights through the system on behalf of others on a daily basis.'],
["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.intro.SectionTitle4Component1ParagraphText",'YouTube and its Content ID partners enter into an agreement that sets the parameters for the use of the tool and allows YouTube to make appropriate use of the copyright owner\'s content for the purpose of enabling Content ID to work. Partners provide YouTube with reference files for the works they own, along with metadata such as the title and detailed ownership rights. Based on these <a target="_blank" href="https://support.google.com/youtube/answer/2605065">references</a>, YouTube creates digital "fingerprints" for the copyright owner\'s works and conducts automated scans of the platform to determine when content in an uploaded video matches the reference content.'],
["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.intro.SectionTitle4Group2Component0ParagraphText","Copyright owners and service providers use YouTube's Studio Content Manager \u2014 the interface for managing the use of their content on the platform \u2014 to instruct the system to either block, monetize, or track matching content. Rightsholders can choose which policy to apply to a claim: they can leave it up and make money from it; leave it up and track viewing statistics; or block it from being viewed. In the first half of 2023, rightsholders chose to monetize over 90% of all Content ID claims. The Studio Content Manager interface provides highly granular access control to assist users who have specific, complex, or even conflicting ownership rights. For example, rights and policies may be specific to certain countries, resulting in a video being monetized in one country and blocked in another."],
["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.intro.SectionTitle4Group2Component0ParagraphTextV6","Copyright owners and service providers use YouTube's Studio Content Manager \u2014 the interface for managing the use of their content on the platform \u2014 to instruct the system to either block, monetize, or track matching content. Rightsholders can choose which policy to apply to a claim: they can leave it up and make money from it; leave it up and track viewing statistics; or block it from being viewed. Between {date1} and {date2}, rightsholders chose to monetize over {monetizedclaimspercent} of all Content ID claims. The Studio Content Manager interface provides highly granular access control to assist users who have specific, complex, or even conflicting ownership rights. For example, rights and policies may be specific to certain countries, resulting in a video being monetized in one country and blocked in another."],
["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.intro.SectionTitle4Group2Component0ParagraphTextV7","Copyright owners and service providers use YouTube's Studio Content Manager \u2014 the interface for managing the use of their content on the platform \u2014 to instruct the system to either block, monetize, or track matching content. Rightsholders can choose which policy to apply to a claim: they can leave it up and make money from it; leave it up and track viewing statistics; or block it from being viewed. In {year1}, rightsholders chose to monetize over {monetizedclaimspercent} of all Content ID claims. The Studio Content Manager interface provides highly granular access control to assist users who have specific, complex, or even conflicting ownership rights. For example, rights and policies may be specific to certain countries, resulting in a video being monetized in one country and blocked in another."],
["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.intro.SectionTitle4Group2Component1ChartTitle","Content ID claims that are monetized"],["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.intro.SectionTitle4Component3ParagraphText","Reference files that reflect flawed or conflicting ownership data can create issues among Content ID partners. Content ID is designed to address these ownership conflicts among partners and manage disputes with uploaders. In some situations, such as when the system is uncertain whether a claim should be made, the system creates a queue of potential claims that require manual review to be activated. As a result, Content ID requires users to make a high level of operational investment, without which other rightsholders could have their rights impaired and lawful expression could be inappropriately impacted."],
["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.access.SectionTitle0Component0ParagraphText","<b>Webform:</b> In the first half of 2023, over 60% of claimants who claimed or requested removal of content through YouTube's Copyright Management Suite did so through the copyright webform, which is designed to be an easy option for those with infrequent needs."],["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.access.SectionTitle0Component0ParagraphTextV6","<b>Webform:</b> Between {date1} and {date2}, over 60% of claimants who claimed or requested removal of content through YouTube's Copyright Management Suite did so through the copyright webform, which is designed to be an easy option for those with infrequent needs."],
["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.access.SectionTitle0Component0ParagraphTextV7","<b>Webform:</b> In {year1}, over 60% of claimants who claimed or requested removal of content through YouTube's Copyright Management Suite did so through the copyright webform, which is designed to be an easy option for those with infrequent needs."],["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.access.SectionTitle0Component1ParagraphText","<b>Copyright Match Tool:</b> As of June 2023, over 2.5 million channels have access to the Copyright Match Tool, which we built for those with more frequent copyright needs. It automatically identifies videos that are matches or potential matches of a user's content. Anybody with a YouTube account who is in the YouTube Partner Program or has submitted a valid copyright takedown notice is eligible for Copyright Match Tool access."],
["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.access.SectionTitle0Component1ParagraphTextV6","<b>Copyright Match Tool:</b> As of {date2}, over {copyrightmatchtoolaccesscount} channels have access to the Copyright Match Tool, which we built for those with more frequent copyright needs. It automatically identifies videos that are matches or potential matches of a user's content. Anybody with a YouTube account who is in the YouTube Partner Program or has submitted a valid copyright takedown notice is eligible for Copyright Match Tool access."],
["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.access.SectionTitle0Component1ParagraphTextV7","<b>Copyright Match Tool:</b> As of {date2}, over {copyrightmatchtoolaccesscount} channels have access to the Copyright Match Tool, which we built for those with more frequent copyright needs. It automatically identifies videos that are matches or potential matches of a user's content. Anybody with a YouTube account who is in the YouTube Partner Program or has submitted a valid copyright takedown notice is eligible for access to the Copyright Match Tool."],
["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.access.SectionTitle0Component1ParagraphTextV8","<b>Copyright Match Tool:</b> As of {date2}, over {copyrightmatchtoolaccesscount} channels have access to the Copyright Match Tool, which we built for those with more frequent copyright needs. It automatically identifies videos that are matches or potential matches of a user's content. Anybody with a YouTube account who is in the YouTube Partner Program or has submitted a valid copyright takedown notice is eligible for access to the Copyright Match Tool."],
["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.access.SectionTitle0Component2ParagraphText","<b>Content ID:</b> Content ID is for enterprise partners (such as movie studios, record labels, and collecting societies) and service providers who operate at an entirely different scale \u2014 over {number} partners have access and many have a dedicated team just to operate the tool. The scale of copyright needs for these partners is in a class all by itself, and their content \u2014 today's hit song, scenes from a new movie or the latest viral video \u2014 is at the heart of creative reuse on YouTube. Claims from Content ID partners represent over 99% of all copyright actions on YouTube."],
["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.access.SectionTitle0Component2ParagraphTextV6","<b>Content ID:</b> Content ID is for enterprise partners (such as movie studios, record labels, and collecting societies) and service providers who operate at an entirely different scale \u2014 over {accessparterncount} partners had access between {date1} and {date2} and many have a dedicated team just to operate the tool. The scale of copyright needs for these partners is in a class all by itself, and their content \u2014 today's hit song, scenes from a new movie or the latest viral video \u2014 is at the heart of creative reuse on YouTube. Claims from Content ID partners represent over 99% of all copyright actions on YouTube."],
["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.access.SectionTitle0Component2ParagraphTextV7","<b>Content ID:</b> Content ID is for enterprise partners (such as movie studios, record labels, and collecting societies) and service providers who operate at an entirely different scale \u2014 over {accessparterncount} partners had access in {year1} and many have a dedicated team just to operate the tool. The scale of copyright needs for these partners is in a class all by itself, and their content \u2014 today's hit song, scenes from a new movie or the latest viral video \u2014 is at the heart of creative reuse on YouTube. Claims from Content ID partners represent over 99% of all copyright actions on YouTube."],
["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.access.SectionTitle0Component2ParagraphTextV8","<b>Content ID:</b> Content ID is for enterprise partners (such as movie studios, record labels, and collecting societies) and service providers who operate at an entirely different scale \u2014 over {accessparterncount} partners had access in {year1} and many have a dedicated team just to operate the tool. The scale of copyright needs for these partners is in a class all by itself, and their content \u2014 today's hit song, scenes from a new movie or the latest viral video \u2014 is at the heart of creative reuse on YouTube. Claims from Content ID partners represent over 99% of all copyright actions on YouTube."],
["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.access.SectionTitle0Component3ParagraphText","A note on terminology: In this report, we distinguish between tools to submit removal requests (the webform and the Copyright Match Tool) and Content ID, which places a claim on a video. 'Claim' will always refer to Content ID actions, whereas 'removal request' will refer to the webform and the Copyright Match Tool."],["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.access.SectionTitle0Component3ParagraphTextV7","<i>A note on terminology: In this report, we distinguish between tools to submit removal requests (the webform and the Copyright Match Tool) and Content ID, which places a claim on a video. 'Claim' will always refer to Content ID actions, whereas 'removal request' will refer to the webform and the Copyright Match Tool.</i>"],
["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.access.SectionTitle1","Eligibility and access"],["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.access.SectionTitle1Component0ParagraphText","Every logged-in YouTube user has access to the webform, which we built to be a streamlined and efficient way to submit removal requests. YouTube has invested considerable resources into making the webform easy to use and effective for the vast majority of use cases."],["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.access.SectionTitle1Component1ParagraphText",
'We automatically offer access to the Copyright Match Tool to channels in the YouTube Partner Program. For these channels, the tool finds potential matches of their uploads to YouTube. In addition, anyone who submits valid copyright removal requests through our webform has access. For these cases, the tool detects and surfaces potential matches of videos removed by the rightsholder. Rightsholders can also apply for all copyright tools via <a target="_blank" href="https://support.google.com/youtube/contact/copyright_management_tools_form">YouTube\'s Copyright Management tools application</a>. The eligibility criteria for each copyright management tool is described in <a target="_blank" href="https://support.google.com/youtube/answer/9245819">YouTube\'s Help Center</a>.'],
["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.access.SectionTitle1Component2ParagraphText","A history of sending YouTube complete and valid copyright removal requests to remove allegedly infringing content serves as the primary indicator that an applicant both needs scaled tools and understands copyright. Abuse of our webform, or otherwise invalid use due to a lack of understanding about copyright, is very common."],["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.access.SectionTitle1Component3ParagraphText","When YouTube responds to an applicant who completes YouTube\u2019s Copyright Management tools application, YouTube always explains the tools available to a given applicant. We also indicate that if the applicant believes they should be eligible for additional tools, they are welcome to apply again in 90 days. In the interim, the best course of action for any rightsholder is to make use of the tools they already have access to. This demonstrates both that they do have a need that is better addressed by a more scaled tool, and that they understand copyright removal processes. If an applicant wishes to appeal the initial decision and provide additional information for us to consider, they may respond directly to the email."],
["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.access.SectionTitle2","Access and usage of copyright tools"],["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.access.SectionTitle2Component0ParagraphText","The following data demonstrates the relationship between how many rightsholders have access to a particular tool, how many use that tool, and the scale of their need to manage their copyright on YouTube. We match rightsholders to the tool that best meets their needs, based in part on how much content on YouTube is subject to their copyright. The webform is available to everyone and is used by more people than any other tool."],
["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.access.SectionTitle2Component1ParagraphText","Those in the YouTube Partner Program, and those who have used our webform successfully, also have access to the Copyright Match Tool, and thus have the benefit of the most requested feature of Content ID \u2014 the ability to automatically detect potentially infringing content. This feature helps users better detect and manage their copyright through a user-friendly interface."],["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.access.SectionTitle2Component2ParagraphText",
"Content ID partners are managing content at an entirely different scale and sophistication. Though they represent the smallest number of users for any of our tools, their actions represent over 99% of all copyright actions on YouTube. This is because their content \u2014 today\u2019s hit song, scenes from a new movie, or the latest viral video \u2014 is at the heart of creative reuse on YouTube and is the most likely to be reuploaded. To keep the ecosystem safe, we regularly evaluate partners' access to CID to ensure they demonstrate an ongoing need for scaled rights management. In some cases, these evaluations may result in removing a partner\u2019s access to Content ID and matching them with a more appropriate copyright management tool."],
["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.access.SectionTitle2Group3","Exhibit 1.1 \u2013 Access and usage of copyright tools"],["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.access.SectionTitle2Group3Component0ChartTitle","Channels on YouTube have access to the Copyright Match Tool"],["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.access.SectionTitle2Group3Component1ParagraphText","This table shows the number of users who have access to each tool as of June 2023, along with how many used the tool in the first half of 2023.<br><br>In some cases, an individual may make copyright removal requests or make claims using multiple tools (for example, if they are granted access to Copyright Match Tool after making successful copyright removal requests via the webform). In these cases, they are counted as users of both tools."],
["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.access.SectionTitle2Group3Component1ParagraphTextV6","This table shows the number of users who have access to each tool as of {date2}, along with how many used the tool between {date1} and {date2}.<br><br>In some cases, an individual may make copyright removal requests or make claims using multiple tools (for example, if they are granted access to Copyright Match Tool after making successful copyright removal requests via the webform). In these cases, they are counted as users of both tools."],
["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.access.SectionTitle2Group3Component1ParagraphTextV7","This table shows the number of users who have access to each tool as of {date1}, along with how many used the tool in {year1}.<br><br>In some cases, an individual may make copyright removal requests or make claims using multiple tools (for example, if they are granted access to the Copyright Match Tool after making successful copyright removal requests via the webform). In these cases, they are counted as users of both tools."],
["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.access.SectionTitle2Group4","Exhibit 1.2 \u2013 Copyright actions by tool"],["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.access.SectionTitle2Group4Component0ChartTitle","Channels on YouTube have access to the Copyright Match Tool"],["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.access.SectionTitle2Group4Component1ParagraphText","This chart shows the number of unique claims and copyright removal requests made in the first half of 2023. Note that a video may be subject to multiple claims or removal requests, such as when it features more than one copyrighted asset or when multiple parties share ownership of copyrighted assets.<br><br>A Content ID claim can convert to a copyright removal request at the claimant's request. In these cases, we have counted them as two separate actions: one Content ID claim and one copyright removal request."],
["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.access.SectionTitle2Group4Component1ParagraphTextV6","This chart shows the number of unique claims and copyright removal requests made between {date1} and {date2}. Note that a video may be subject to multiple claims or removal requests, such as when it features more than one copyrighted asset or when multiple parties share ownership of copyrighted assets.<br><br>A Content ID claim can convert to a copyright removal request at the claimant's request. In these cases, we have counted them as two separate actions: one Content ID claim and one copyright removal request."],
["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.access.SectionTitle2Group4Component1ParagraphTextV7","This chart shows the number of unique claims and copyright removal requests made in {year1}. Note that a video may be subject to multiple claims or removal requests, such as when it features more than one copyrighted asset or when multiple parties share ownership of copyrighted assets.<br><br>A Content ID claim can convert to a copyright removal request at the claimant's request. In these cases, we have counted them as two separate actions: one Content ID claim and one copyright removal request."],
["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.ecosystem.SectionTitle0Component0ParagraphText","YouTube takes steps to prevent abuse or otherwise invalid use of our rights management tools. We also empower uploaders to push back on requests and claims they believe are wrongful. As demonstrated by our public copyright webform, when we make a tool available to attempted abuse. This is why we must balance access to tools against the significant disruptions to the ecosystem they can cause."],["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.ecosystem.SectionTitle0Group1Component0ParagraphText",
"Over 6% of videos requested for removal through the public webform in the first half of 2023 were the subject of abusive copyright removal requests, meaning these requests were assessed and rejected by our review team as a likely false assertion of copyright ownership. The attempted abuse rate through the webform is more than 10 times higher than the attempted abuse rate across all other copyright removal tools."],["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.ecosystem.SectionTitle0Group1Component0ParagraphTextV6",
"Over 6% of videos requested for removal through the public webform between {date1} and {date2} were the subject of abusive copyright removal requests, meaning these requests were assessed and rejected by our review team as a likely false assertion of copyright ownership. The attempted abuse rate through the webform was more than {webformabusemagnification} times higher between {date1} and {date2} than the attempted abuse rate across all other copyright removal tools."],["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.ecosystem.SectionTitle0Group1Component0ParagraphTextV7",
"Over 6% of videos requested for removal through the public webform in {year1} were the subject of abusive copyright removal requests, meaning these requests were assessed and rejected by our review team as a likely false assertion of copyright ownership. The attempted abuse rate through the webform was more than {webformabusemagnification} times higher than the attempted abuse rate across all other copyright removal tools."],["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.ecosystem.SectionTitle0Group1Component1ChartTitle",
"Higher attempted abuse rate in webform than in copyright removal tools with limited access"],["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.ecosystem.SectionTitle0Component2ParagraphText","The limited availability of Content ID also helps to limit abuse of that tool. This is especially important because claiming can happen automatically. While a single copyright removal requested from the webform impacts only one (or a handful) of videos, <b>a single invalid reference file in Content ID can impact thousands of videos and users</b>, stripping them of monetization or blocking them altogether."],
["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.ecosystem.SectionTitle0Component3ParagraphText","Uploaders are empowered to push back on removals they believe are invalid by filing counter notifications. They can also push back on Content ID claims by disputing the claim. Like abuse, pushback from uploaders occurs most often in response to actions from tools that are available to more people. In the first half of 2023, uploaders submitted counter notifications in response to over 5% of removal requests submitted via the webform, whereas it\u2019s fewer than 2% for both Enterprise Webform and for Copyright Match Tool. Fewer than 1% of all Content ID claims made in the first half of 2023 have been disputed. Over 65% of those disputes succeeded because claimants either voluntarily released the claim or did not respond to the dispute in time."],
["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.ecosystem.SectionTitle0Component3ParagraphTextV6","Uploaders are empowered to push back on removals they believe are invalid by filing counter notifications. They can also push back on Content ID claims by disputing the claim. Like abuse, pushback from uploaders occurs most often in response to actions from tools that are available to more people. Between {date1} and {date2}, uploaders submitted counter notifications in response to over {removalrequestpercent} of removal requests submitted via the webform, whereas it\u2019s fewer than 2% for both the Enterprise Webform and for the Copyright Match Tool. Fewer than 1% of all Content ID claims made between {date1} and {date2} have been disputed. Over 65% of those disputes succeeded because claimants either voluntarily released the claim or did not respond to the dispute in time."],
["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.ecosystem.SectionTitle0Component3ParagraphTextV7","Uploaders are empowered to push back on removals they believe are invalid by filing counter notifications. They can also push back on Content ID claims by disputing the claim. Like abuse, pushback from uploaders occurs most often in response to actions from tools that are available to more people. In {year1}, uploaders submitted counter notifications in response to over {percent1} of removal requests submitted via the webform, whereas it\u2019s fewer than {percent2} for both the Enterprise Webform and for the Copyright Match Tool. Fewer than 1% of all Content ID claims made in {year1} have been disputed. Over {percent3} of those disputes succeeded because claimants either voluntarily released the claim or did not respond to the dispute in time."],
["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.ecosystem.SectionTitle0Component3ParagraphTextV8","Uploaders are empowered to push back on removals they believe are invalid by filing counter notifications. They can also push back on Content ID claims by disputing the claim. Like abuse, pushback from uploaders occurs most often in response to actions from tools that are available to more people. In {year1}, uploaders submitted counter notifications in response to over {percent1} of removal requests submitted via the webform, whereas it\u2019s {percent2} or less for both the Enterprise Webform and for the Copyright Match Tool. Fewer than 1% of all Content ID claims made in {year1} have been disputed. Over {percent3} of those disputes succeeded because claimants either voluntarily released the claim or did not respond to the dispute in time."],
["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.ecosystem.SectionTitle1","Invalid requests and abuse"],["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.ecosystem.SectionTitle1Group0Component0ParagraphText","<b>Invalid requests</b> often occur when someone incorrectly submits a copyright removal request due to a lack of understanding of either copyright law or our tools. Invalid requests can take many forms, such as filing a copyright request for a trademark or privacy issue or simply failing to provide the necessary information required to constitute a valid copyright removal request. For Content ID partners, invalid claims occur when partners unintentionally deliver low-quality reference files that contain incomplete metadata, insufficiently unique content, or pieces of content that they don't own exclusively \u2014 such as a news broadcast that contains public domain footage or a song that uses a licensed beat."],
["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.ecosystem.SectionTitle1Group0Component1ParagraphText","<b>Abuse</b>, on the other hand, occurs when someone is deemed by our review team to have intentionally and maliciously used our tools in an attempt to remove content from YouTube through a likely false assertion of copyright ownership. Sometimes this takes the form of political actors attempting to censor political speech or companies stifling criticism of their products or practices. Other times individuals try to use our copyright processes to bully other creators or to remove videos they see as competing for the same audience."],
["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.ecosystem.SectionTitle1Component1ParagraphText","Regardless of the intent, both invalid requests and abuse can cause significant disruptions to all members of the YouTube ecosystem \u2014 creators, viewers, and rightsholders. Just one bad copyright webform notice can result in multiple videos being temporarily removed from YouTube. In Content ID the impact is multiplied due to its automated nature; one bad reference file can impact hundreds or even thousands of videos across the site. In one highly publicized instance, a news channel uploaded public domain footage from NASA of a Mars rover and ended up making inappropriate claims against all other news channels and creators using the same footage, even against the NASA channel itself."],
["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.ecosystem.SectionTitle1Component2ParagraphText","We have dedicated teams working to detect and prevent abuse or otherwise invalid use of each of our tools. We rely on a combination of humans and technology to detect suspicious behavior, request additional information where necessary, and remove reference files that are low-quality or invalid. We take abuse of our tools seriously \u2014 we terminate tens of thousands of accounts each year that attempt to abuse our copyright tools."],
["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.ecosystem.SectionTitle1Group3","Common types of abusers"],["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.ecosystem.SectionTitle1Group3Component0ParagraphText","<b>Impersonator</b><br>Uses a fake name or email address to get away with submitting fake requests"],["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.ecosystem.SectionTitle1Group3Component1ParagraphText","<b>Reputation manager</b><br>Uses Copyright webform to remove allegedly defamatory content"],["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.ecosystem.SectionTitle1Group3Component2ParagraphText",
"<b>Competitor</b><br>Submits requests on legitimate content to target their competitors"],["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.ecosystem.SectionTitle1Group3Component3ParagraphText","<b>Backdater</b><br>Pretends to have created content first (backdates) in order to remove other people's content"],["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.ecosystem.SectionTitle2","YouTube's responses to copyright removal requests"],["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.ecosystem.SectionTitle2Component0ParagraphText",
"Because it is available to everyone, our copyright webform is subject to a significant amount of abuse or otherwise invalid use. We have a team dedicated to processing copyright removal requests. When necessary, we request additional information or deny the request altogether. Below you can see real world examples, anonymized for the report, of how we responded to removal requests we received."],["transparencyReport.youtubecopyright.ecosystem.SectionTitle2Component1ChartTitle","Possible responses by YouTube to a copyright removal request, with examples"],
